benchmarks/sat/satcomp2024-sample/*.drat
benchmarks/sat/unsat/*.drat

# Default proof-carrying certificates (proof-carrying is ON BY DEFAULT).
# These are the auto-generated sidecars AY writes next to an input file on an
# UNSAT/refutation verdict: `<input>.drat` (SAT), `<input>.alethe` (SMT),
# `<input>.chccert` + `chc-obligations/` (CHC). They are regenerable and must
# never pollute the tree when tests or users solve repo fixtures in place. The
# `<input>.<ext>` shape means an explicit `--proof out.alethe` (a bare name the
# user chose) is NOT matched and can still be committed with `git add -f`.
*.cnf.drat
*.dimacs.drat
*.smt2.alethe
*.smt.alethe
*.smt2.chccert
*.smt.chccert
# Atomic proof writers create these beside the final artifact. A killed solver
# can leave them behind, and large SMT proofs can exceed GitHub's file limit.
*.drat.tmp-*
*.lrat.tmp-*
*.alethe.tmp-*
*.lean4.tmp-*
*.chccert.tmp-*
# The CHC certificate writer takes a lock beside the artifact as a DOT-prefixed
# sibling: `.<input>.chccert.ay-chc.lock`. The `*.smt2.chccert` rule above does
# not match it (the name continues past `.chccert`), so without this line every
# test run that touches a `benchmarks/smt` fixture leaves untracked files behind.
.*.chccert.ay-chc.lock
*.pbp.tmp-*
*.proof-artifact.json.tmp.*
chc-obligations/
# ...except the committed DRAT canary fixtures (checker regression inputs).
!benchmarks/sat/canary/*.cnf.drat
